"[2609.10149] Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10149)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10149\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 9 Sep 2026]\nL9: # Title:Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Zhantong Xue】, 【6†Pingchuan Ma】, 【7†Zhaoyu Wang】, 【8†Yuguang Zhou】, 【9†Huaijin Wang】, 【10†Shuai Wang】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits, by Zhantong Xue and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【11†View PDF】 【12†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Zero-knowledge (ZK) proof systems for neural-network inference compile the model into a system of arithmetic constraints. Many of these constraints are redundant checks: range proofs, sign lookups, and bit decompositions who are globally entailed by the rest of the circuit through chains of reasoning that span distant gadgets.\nL17: Removing them shrinks the circuit and accelerates proving, but the removal must be carefully justified: an unsoundly debloated circuit becomes forgeable, accepting witnesses the original would have rejected and so allowing a prover to claim, for example, that a neural network produced an output it never actually computed. Such soundness vulnerabilities are not hypothetical: under-constrained circuits in deployed ZK systems have enabled attackers to forge transactions and bypass verification entirely.\nL18: > We present an automated framework that removes redundant checks while provably preserving soundness. For each candidate removal, our tool first checks whether the rest of the circuit, on its own, can still rule out every value the removed check was excluding.\nL19: Using whole-circuit abstract interpretation, the analysis searches for such alternative justifications and records them in a provenance graph; a check is then removed only when an alternative path through the graph still derives the facts that it is checking. This ensures that the debloated circuit opens no new forging strategy to an adversary.\nL20: We evaluate circuits spanning MLP, CNN, RNN, and transformer architectures generated by two production frameworks (ezkl and zkml), with up to 25.3 million constraints. Our tool removes up to 48.7\\% of constraints and reduces prover time by up to 72.8\\%, without weakening security.\nL21: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【13†arXiv:2609.10149】 [cs.CR]\nL23:    | (or 【14†arXiv:2609.10149v1】 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL24:    | 【15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2609.10149†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Zhantong Xue [【16†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 9 Sep 2026 13:27:25 UTC (105 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits, by Zhantong Xue and 5 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【11†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【12†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【17†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【18†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * 【19†Audio Summary】\nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.CR\nL48: \nL49: 【20†< prev】   |   【21†next >】\nL50: \nL51: 【22†new】 | 【23†recent】 | 【24†2026-09】\nL52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: 【25†cs】\nL56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * 【26†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL60:   * 【27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL61:   * 【28†Semant