149v1 [cs.CR] 09 Sep 2026\nL55: # Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits Note: This is the authors’ version for ACM CCS 2026, with additional appendices. Conference version: Zhantong Xue, Pingchuan Ma, Zhaoyu Wang, Yuguang Zhou, Huaijin Wang, and Shuai Wang. 2026. Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits. In Proceedings of the 2026 ACM SIGSAC Conference on Computer and Communications Security (CCS ’26), November 15–19, 2026, The Hague, Netherlands. ACM.\nL56: Forthcoming. Assigned DOI: 【43†https://doi.org/10.1145/3830454.3846774†doi.org】. © 2026 the authors. This manuscript is licensed under 【44†CC BY 4.0†creativecommons.org】.\nL57: CCS: Security and privacy Cryptography CCS: Theory of computation Program analysis\nL58: Zhantong Xue Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Hong Kong, China email: zxueai@cse.ust.hk , Pingchuan Ma Note: Corresponding author.\nL59: Affiliation: Zhejiang University of Technology, Hangzhou, China email: pma@zjut.edu.cn , Zhaoyu Wang Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Hong Kong, China email: zwangjz@cse.ust.hk , Yuguang Zhou Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Hong Kong, China email: yzhougv@cse.ust.hk , Huaijin Wang Affiliation: Shandong University, Jinan, China email: huaijinwang@sdu.edu.cn and Shuai Wang Affiliation: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Hong Kong, China email: shuaiw@cse.ust.hk\nL60: © none\nL61: ###### Abstract.\nL62: Zero-knowledge (ZK) proof systems for neural-network inference compile the model into a system of arithmetic constraints. Many of these constraints are redundant checks: range proofs, sign lookups, and bit decompositions who are globally entailed by the rest of the circuit through chains of reasoning that span distant gadgets.\nL63: Removing them shrinks the circuit and accelerates proving, but the removal must be carefully justified: an unsoundly debloated circuit becomes forgeable, accepting witnesses the original would have rejected and so allowing a prover to claim, for example, that a neural network produced an output it never actually computed. Such soundness vulnerabilities are not hypothetical: under-constrained circuits in deployed ZK systems have enabled attackers to forge transactions and bypass verification entirely.\nL64: We present an automated framework that removes redundant checks while provably preserving soundness. For each candidate removal, our tool first checks whether the rest of the circuit, on its own, can still rule out every value the removed check was excluding.\nL65: Using whole-circuit abstract interpretation, the analysis searches for such alternative justifications and records them in a provenance graph; a check is then removed only when an alternative path through the graph still derives the facts that it is checking. This ensures that the debloated circuit opens no new forging strategy to an adversary.\nL66: We evaluate circuits spanning MLP, CNN, RNN, and transformer architectures generated by two production frameworks (ezkl and zkml), with up to 25.3 million constraints. Our tool removes up to 48.7% of constraints and reduces prover time by up to 72.8%, without weakening security.\nL67: ###### Keywords:\nL68: \nL69: zero-knowledge proofs, circuit soundness, constraint redundancy, machine learning\nL70: ## 1. Introduction\nL71: Zero-knowledge (ZK) proof systems (【45†Goldwasser et al., 1985】) let a prover convince a verifier that a computation was performed correctly without revealing its inputs. The computation is encoded as a constraint system $\\mathcal{C}$, a quantifier-free conjunction of polynomial equations over a finite field; the prover’s secret witness $w$ is a satisfying assignment, $w\\models\\mathcal{C}$.\nL72: A fast-growing application is zero-knowledge machine learning: a framework compiles inference of a trained network into such a constraint system, so that a witness satisfies $\\mathcal{C}$ exactly when it encodes a correct inference. A proof of knowledge of such a witness therefore certifies the inference while revealing neither the input nor the model weights (【46†Peng et al., 2026】), enabling on-chain AI verification and privacy-preserving model serving.\nL73: Prover cost scales with the number of constraints. Even small models compile to circuits with tens to hundreds of thousands of constraints, and proving a single inference can take minutes to hours (【47†Kang et al., 2022】; 【48†South et al., 2024】). Recent benchmarks report proving overhead of $10{,}000\\times$ or more over plain inference, with hundreds of gigabytes of RAM consumed for moderate-sized models (【49†Modulus Labs, 2023】).\nL74: Shrinking the constraint system is thus a natural target for reducing this cost.\nL75: A major cause of this overhead, accounting for up to 48.7% of constraints in our evaluation (§【20†6】), is the large number and diversity of well-formedness checks that ZK machine learning (ML) frameworks emit on intermediate variables, including range checks on activation outputs, sign lookups for value decompositions, remainder bounds in integer division gadgets, and bit-decomposition validity proofs (【48†South et al., 2024】; 【50†Chen et al., 2024】).\nL76: Like assertions in program verification, checks compute no value; they only restrict the admissible assignments, guarding against a malicious prover supplying an out-of-range witness. A gadget is a reusable subcircuit implementing a single operation, such as an activation or division. Yet composition makes many of these checks redundant: each gadget is compiled as a self-contained template carrying every check needed for any input it might receive in isolation.\nL77: When the framework wires gadgets together, an upstream check often constrains a shared variable to a range that already implies a downstream check on the same variable.\nL78: For example, the constraint emitted by a ReLU gadget bounds its output $z$ to $[0,R_{\\mathsf{relu}}]$, while the constraint from the next layer’s range check independently bounds the same $z$ to $[0,R_{\\mathsf{rng}}]$ with $R_{\\mathsf{rng}}\\leq R_{\\mathsf{relu}}$; the upstream constraint still asserts a valid bound, but that bound is already implied by the tighter downstream one.\nL79: Such compositional redundancies pervade ML circuits because the same gadget templates are instantiated hundreds of times across layers (§【11†3】). This raises a natural question:\nL80: > Can we automatically identify and remove (i.e., debloat^{1}^{1} 1 Analogous to software debloating, which removes unused code from binaries.) these redundant checks to reduce proving cost, without compromising security?\nL81: Debloating & ZK ML security. Answering this requires defining what makes debloating safe.\nL82: We borrow “sound” from ZK proof systems, where it forbids any prover from convincing the verifier of a false statement: a debloated constraint set $\\mathcal{C}^{\\prime}\\subseteq\\mathcal{C}$ is sound when every witness it accepts is also accepted by the original ($w\\models\\mathcal{C}^{\\prime}$ implies $w\\models\\mathcal{C}$); equivalently, every removed check $c$ is entailed by the survivors, i.e., a logical consequence of them ($\\mathcal{C}^{\\prime}\\vdash c$), leaving the model set unchanged.\nL83: Unsound debloating enlarges the accepted-witness set, producing exactly the under-constrained circuits behind real-world ZK exploits (【51†Aztec, 2022】; 【52†NIST, 2019】). As Fig. 【53†1】 illustrates, this lets a malicious prover supply a forged witness for the model $\\mathcal{M}$ on input $x$, producing a proof $\\pi$ that the verifier still accepts and claiming inferences $\\mathcal{M}$ never produced. Such attacks evade detection, since $\\pi$ is the only artifact the verifier ever sees.\nL84: Soundness is therefore critical for any optimisation that modifies the constraint set.\nL85: Figure 1. Threat model: unsound debloating enables a forged proof being accepted by the verifier.A two-row flow diagram. A model enters a framework that produces circuit C, and a debloater transforms it into an unsoundly debloated circuit C prime. C prime is published to a malicious prover and a victim verifier. The prover creates a forged proof that satisfies C prime, causing the verifier to accept incorrectly.\nL86: Challenges. We observe that soundly eliminating redundant checks is challenging for ZK ML frameworks. First, redundancy is inherently global: a check that looks necessary in a local gadget may be implied by constraints several layers away, with the implication flowing in either direction across the circuit, so no per-gadget or single-direction analysis suffices. Second, redundancy does not compose.\nL87: Two checks may each be individually redundant yet unsound to remove together, because each derivation of redundancy uses the other removed check as a premise; we call this circularity the premise-asymmetry problem (§【14†3.3】). Third, exact reasoning is intractable: each redundancy query $\\mathcal{C}\\setminus\\{c\\}\\vdash c$ is an entailment in nonlinear field arithmetic (the analogue of QF_NIA), already co-NP-hard for a single constraint (§【14†3.3】), and the largest sound removal is harder still.\nL88: Our solution. This semantic redundancy, checks that are active and unique yet entailed by the rest of the circuit, is invisible to existing optimizers, which decide removals from the syntactic or algebraic form of constraints alone (§【10†2.3】); on our benchmarks they remove at most 0.4% (§【24†6.4】). We give, to our knowledge, the first sound and scalable method for this class. The technical core is a provenance-tracked proof search.\nL89: We pair abstract interpretation (【54†Cousot and Cousot, 1977】), a tractable analysis that derives sound facts (abstract properties such as range bounds and known bit patterns) about each variable, with a provenance graph, a proof log recording, for every derived fact, the constraints and premises that derived it.\nL90: A check is removed only when every fact it enforces remains derivable from the remaining constraints, decided by a proof search over the graph; cycle detection in the search rules out the circular mutual derivations that premise asymmetry would otherwise allow. The successful search itself certifies the soundness of each removal covered by the entailment rules.\nL91: In our evaluation (§【20†6】), the tool eliminates 25.5% of constraints on average (up to 48.7%) and reduces proving time by 33.4% on average (up to 72.8%).\nL92: Contributions. In sum, we make the following contributions:\nL93: \nL94:   * •\nL95: \nL96: We identify the well-formedness checks emitted by ZK machine learning frameworks as a systematic source of semantic redundancy untapped by existing optimizers, and formalize the three challenges that make its sound removal non-trivial.\nL97: \nL98:   * •\nL99: We propose an abstract interpretation framework that derives statically checkable facts about each variable and tracks the provenance of each fact back to the constraints that proved it. The construction is sound: for each removed constraint, no malicious prover gains additional power from the removal.\nL100: \nL101:   * •\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats · GitHub (https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 235\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WOR